terms that require scrutiny.\n\nQ1 2026's $2.26B OCF against a -32% operating margin is arithmetically implausible without a massive working capital source — specifically, advance customer payments or deferred revenue. If large cloud customers pre-paid for capacity (common in hyperscaler buildouts), this OCF will reverse as services are delivered, converting future quarters' OCF sharply negative. This creates a cliff effect: reported OCF looks strong now but becomes a headwind over the next 2-4 quarters, precisely when CapEx obligations will remain elevated.\n\nAnalyst consensus (Buy, $196 PT) is already below the current $220 price, and the DCF model produces a deeply negative intrinsic value (-$80), reflecting that at current burn rates and without a clear GM recovery timeline, the equity value is a pure option on future profitability. The macro backdrop (10Y at 4.56%, yield curve spread +40bps) adds refinancing risk: if NBIS needs to roll or add debt in 2026-2027, higher-for-longer rates compress the economics of debt-financed GPU infrastructure materially.\n\nIn summary: the company has adequate nominal liquidity for 2-3 quarters, but the combination of opaque debt terms, structural gross margin deterioration, CapEx running at 6x revenue, and almost certain OCF normalization creates a credible liquidity stress scenario within 12-18 months absent equity issuance or a dramatic revenue/margin recovery.",
